1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,000
Mighty cranes. Divers who push the limits of human endurance.

2
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:12,000
Robotic mini-subs, outfitted with lights, cameras, and powerful claws.

3
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:17,000
Their job, scour the ocean floor to recover crashed airplanes and sunken ships.

4
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:21,000
From the wreck of a jumbo jet to Pearl Harbor heroics,

5
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:24,000
to the retrieval of a lost hydrogen bomb.

6
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:28,000
Now, deep sea salvage on modern marvels.

7
00:00:48,660 --> 00:00:53,820
Imagine being part construction worker, part emergency repairman, and part disaster response team member.

8
00:00:53,821 --> 00:01:02,660
Now imagine yourself underwater, where you'll be wearing heavy equipment while being subjected to crushing physical and psychological pressure.

9
00:01:04,640 --> 00:01:06,780
You might even be in a combat zone.

10
00:01:08,940 --> 00:01:13,900
Or under the watchful eyes of those who consider you a trespasser, or possibly dinner.

11
00:01:15,380 --> 00:01:17,640
Such is the world of the Navy salvage diver.

12
00:01:17,641 --> 00:01:24,820
It's a career only for those willing to plunge into dangerous situations,

13
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:33,900
to perform hard and exhausting work, often in rough seas, cold water, and unpredictable circumstances.

14
00:01:35,420 --> 00:01:41,080
Candidates undergo their initial training at the Navy Diving and Salvage Training Center in Panama City, Florida.

15
00:01:42,460 --> 00:01:47,020
NDSTC overall is responsible for DOD military dive training.

16
00:01:47,021 --> 00:01:53,800
We train from five services, including the Coast Guard, Marine Corps, Navy, Army, and Air Force as well.

17
00:01:54,480 --> 00:02:00,860
We let them know right up front that we're about to challenge you in a mental aspect, in a physical aspect, in a teamwork aspect.

18
00:02:01,820 --> 00:02:09,460
The 155,000 square foot facility has a large pool that is used for physical training and the development of scuba skills.

19
00:02:10,620 --> 00:02:16,360
The tower on the property houses a tank where students can begin to make deeper dives in a controlled environment.

20
00:02:17,020 --> 00:02:22,260
The purpose of this facility is we take our students, we put them in the water, they go

21
00:02:22,261 --> 00:02:28,020
down to 45 feet and the primary thing that they do is work on the rate of descent so

22
00:02:28,021 --> 00:02:31,740
they can go down at a proper rate and even more importantly they want to come up at a

23
00:02:31,741 --> 00:02:36,400
certain travel rate which is 30 feet per minute so that they can experience the pressure differences

24
00:02:36,401 --> 00:02:40,220
from the surface and what's going to be like in the open ocean so they come up and they

25
00:02:40,221 --> 00:02:45,360
don't get any kind of adverse effects from coming up too fast.

26
00:02:45,361 --> 00:02:50,280
In other tanks, students perform various tasks underwater to test their ability to function

27
00:02:50,281 --> 00:02:53,960
in an alien environment while wearing heavy diving gear.

28
00:02:53,961 --> 00:03:02,240
They learn to use hand tools, underwater power tools, and welding and cutting equipment.

29
00:03:02,241 --> 00:03:07,060
The cutting tool that we use is the Broco Exothermic Cutting Torch.

30
00:03:07,061 --> 00:03:09,200
And it has a magnesium core in it.

31
00:03:09,201 --> 00:03:13,600
With the oxygen that's supplied to it, it burns at over 10,000 degrees so that you can

32
00:03:13,601 --> 00:03:17,220
cut metal, rock, concrete, whatever could be in the way.

33
00:03:18,640 --> 00:03:22,560
Today's students have the advantage of technologically advanced underwater equipment.

34
00:03:23,380 --> 00:03:26,422
The Mark 21 dive helmet is standard issue now.

35
00:03:26,423 --> 00:03:29,200
It has a fiberglass shell that is weighted

36
00:03:29,201 --> 00:03:35,160
for neutral buoyancy, and a large faceplate made of the world's strongest polycarbonate plastic.

37
00:03:37,180 --> 00:03:40,980
This is the helmet salvage divers currently wear in almost all situations,

38
00:03:40,981 --> 00:03:45,980
down to the Navy's imposed diving limit of 300 feet.

39
00:03:45,981 --> 00:03:52,980
The Navy uses remotely operated vehicles for dives that are too deep or too dangerous for divers.

40
00:03:52,981 --> 00:03:57,980
These ROVs can also be used to assist divers.

41
00:03:57,981 --> 00:04:03,980
U.S. Navy ROVs are housed at Phoenix International in Maryland.

42
00:04:03,981 --> 00:04:08,980
Phoenix International is our deep ocean search and recovery contractor.

43
00:04:08,981 --> 00:04:11,560
So they husband our equipment.

44
00:04:11,900 --> 00:04:14,300
We call it government-owned contractor operating.

45
00:04:14,740 --> 00:04:18,080
So the Navy owns the ROVs and the search systems.

46
00:04:18,340 --> 00:04:21,500
They maintain them, and then they operate them as we need to call them out.

47
00:04:22,520 --> 00:04:27,600
The ROVs can be as small as a suitcase or as big as an SUV.

48
00:04:28,780 --> 00:04:33,640
The Deep Drone is one of the more powerful ROVs, able to work much deeper than divers.

49
00:04:33,641 --> 00:04:42,760
With D-Drone, we take a 9,200-foot spool of Kevlar jacketed cable where all of the power,

50
00:04:43,400 --> 00:04:47,800
sonar, video signals come through, terminate into the maintenance van.

51
00:04:48,420 --> 00:04:53,480
In addition to these two components, we'll take the maintenance hut and the operator

52
00:04:53,481 --> 00:04:57,620
hut where two people can operate the vehicle.

53
00:04:58,380 --> 00:05:00,820
The pilot controls the drone's thrusters.

54
00:05:03,640 --> 00:05:06,760
along with the lights and video cameras.

55
00:05:08,140 --> 00:05:10,300
The co-pilot operates the sonar,

56
00:05:11,140 --> 00:05:13,540
and he's also at the manipulator station.

57
00:05:14,320 --> 00:05:16,020
There are seven function manipulators

58
00:05:16,021 --> 00:05:18,340
that closely resemble the human arm.

59
00:05:18,480 --> 00:05:22,960
They have seven functions, the shoulder, the forearm, the wrist.

60
00:05:23,740 --> 00:05:27,300
It can rotate for us to be able to put shackles together

61
00:05:27,301 --> 00:05:28,760
to tie into lift lines,

62
00:05:29,480 --> 00:05:33,120
and each arm can lift 250 pounds each in air.

63
00:05:33,640 --> 00:05:40,820
Each salvage job presents its own set of problems, and when the Navy gets involved, it's up to

64
00:05:40,821 --> 00:05:45,880
the supervisor of salvage, based in Washington, D.C., to determine what assets need to be

65
00:05:45,881 --> 00:05:51,880
deployed to accomplish the task.

66
00:05:51,881 --> 00:05:58,400
In 1996, the search and recovery following the mysterious crash of TWA Flight 800, eight

67
00:05:58,401 --> 00:06:03,080
miles off the coast of Long Island, brought many Navy salvage capabilities together.

68
00:06:04,140 --> 00:06:11,400
226 Navy divers, a variety of ROVs, and the ships necessary to deploy them, assisted in

69
00:06:11,401 --> 00:06:16,520
what turned out to be the longest and most expensive airline accident investigation in

70
00:06:16,521 --> 00:06:17,280
American history.

71
00:06:18,240 --> 00:06:24,380
The Navy was working for the National Transportation Safety Board on this operation, who of course

72
00:06:24,381 --> 00:06:26,840
was also working hand-in-hand with the FBI,

73
00:06:27,160 --> 00:06:29,520
because there were many theories initially

74
00:06:29,521 --> 00:06:33,000
about what might have brought TWA Flight 800 down,

75
00:06:33,340 --> 00:06:37,260
including a terrorist attack, a bombing on the aircraft,

76
00:06:37,440 --> 00:06:41,080
or there was also the theory that possibly a Navy vessel

77
00:06:41,081 --> 00:06:43,660
had accidentally shot her out of the sky

78
00:06:43,661 --> 00:06:45,240
with a surface-to-air missile.

79
00:06:46,020 --> 00:06:48,200
There was a mid-air explosion of the aircraft,

80
00:06:48,520 --> 00:06:51,180
which basically took the nose of the aircraft off.

81
00:06:51,181 --> 00:06:55,640
that hit the water, and the plane still under power went in.

82
00:06:56,340 --> 00:06:59,960
And what that did is created a very large debris field.

83
00:07:02,080 --> 00:07:05,140
Most of the wreckage was in about 120 feet of water,

84
00:07:05,860 --> 00:07:07,240
within easy reach of divers.

85
00:07:07,820 --> 00:07:11,300
As a Navy diver, I made several dives during the operation.

86
00:07:12,300 --> 00:07:15,400
And diving on an aircraft accident

87
00:07:15,401 --> 00:07:18,060
is one of the hardest things that we have to do as a Navy diver.

88
00:07:18,061 --> 00:07:25,720
You have all this twisted, very thin metal, the wreckage of the fuselage, which is like razor blades.

89
00:07:25,920 --> 00:07:31,780
And then on top of that, you have miles and miles and miles of electrical wire that was part of the aircraft.

90
00:07:32,260 --> 00:07:36,280
So you're constantly having to watch where you step, where you put your hand.

91
00:07:36,460 --> 00:07:40,360
You don't want to get tangled up in all that electrical wire.

92
00:07:40,580 --> 00:07:46,420
You don't want to cut open your air hose or your umbilical on some of that razor-sharp, twisted metal.

93
00:07:46,421 --> 00:07:53,400
but you still want to move as quickly as possible because you know that your job is so important,

94
00:07:54,120 --> 00:08:00,160
not only for the investigation, but more so for the families and friends of the victims.

95
00:08:00,500 --> 00:08:07,900
And our first job was trying to recover the victims of this horrible tragedy.

96
00:08:09,020 --> 00:08:11,640
The ROVs, with their lights and cameras,

97
00:08:11,641 --> 00:08:16,000
offered valuable assistance to the divers during the victim recovery phase of the operation.

98
00:08:16,420 --> 00:08:21,140
What we did with the MR-1 vehicle was it was kind of used like a bird dog.

99
00:08:21,280 --> 00:08:25,240
We would put it underwater, we would search through the area of wreckage for passengers,

100
00:08:25,980 --> 00:08:28,180
and then when we found someone, the vehicle would stay there,

101
00:08:28,280 --> 00:08:31,940
the diver would go underwater, follow the electrical umbilical down to the vehicle,

102
00:08:32,500 --> 00:08:33,900
and then we'd cover the crash victim.

103
00:08:35,160 --> 00:08:37,640
During airline disaster recovery operations,

104
00:08:38,320 --> 00:08:42,660
finding the flight recorders, referred to as the black boxes, is another priority.

105
00:08:43,620 --> 00:08:48,080
A small acoustical beacon, called a pinger, is attached to each of them.

106
00:08:49,080 --> 00:08:53,860
The way these devices work is that there are actually a couple of small electrical contacts on the end,

107
00:08:54,300 --> 00:08:58,220
which are shorted out when it's immersed in salt water, and it starts chirping.

108
00:09:00,900 --> 00:09:05,940
Those small chirps of sound are what people track or try to locate when one of these devices are underwater.

109
00:09:05,941 --> 00:09:15,780
To find Flight 800's black boxes, divers and ROVs used underwater microphones to home in on the fingers.

110
00:09:18,340 --> 00:09:24,720
I was on board the grass, and actually what ended up happening was we had an underwater vehicle in the water,

111
00:09:24,860 --> 00:09:27,220
and the lights from the vehicle actually lit up the box.

112
00:09:29,280 --> 00:09:32,180
Because even though they're called black boxes, they're actually bright orange.

113
00:09:32,181 --> 00:09:41,320
After the recovery of the black boxes and all 230 victims, divers and ROVs began collecting pieces of the wreckage.

114
00:09:42,080 --> 00:09:48,820
Because of the controversy surrounding the crash, the goal was to retrieve as much of the aircraft as possible to help determine the cause.

115
00:09:49,740 --> 00:09:56,160
We used the big ROVs to tie into portions of the aircraft, like the wings or the cockpit section.

116
00:09:56,700 --> 00:10:00,740
We'd actually run straps or rigging around those pieces.

117
00:10:00,741 --> 00:10:07,240
we'd tie lift lines in from the big salvage ships, pull those pieces onto the ship and

118
00:10:07,241 --> 00:10:11,980
send them back to the beach on barges.

119
00:10:11,981 --> 00:10:16,300
As the salvage teams recovered more and more of the aircraft, they began to eliminate a

120
00:10:16,301 --> 00:10:19,500
number of sinister scenarios.

121
00:10:19,501 --> 00:10:25,720
We fortunately had diving with us in our scuba teams were our explosive ordnance disposal

122
00:10:25,721 --> 00:10:26,820
experts.

123
00:10:26,821 --> 00:10:29,660
These people know what they're looking at and what they're looking for.

124
00:10:30,240 --> 00:10:34,560
And there was never anything recovered from the debris field

125
00:10:34,561 --> 00:10:40,420
that could possibly have been part of a bomb, a missile, or an explosive device of any sort.

126
00:10:40,780 --> 00:10:43,480
So in our minds, we knew that that was not a possibility.

127
00:10:44,480 --> 00:10:48,840
By the end of the salvage mission, 98% of the plane was recovered.

128
00:10:49,640 --> 00:10:53,420
In a hangar on Long Island, workers pieced the wreckage back together

129
00:10:53,421 --> 00:10:55,420
to help determine the cause of the crash.

130
00:10:56,820 --> 00:11:07,858
The current best conclusion is that there was a spark in a pump in the fuel tank that ignited due to a whole series of factors that caused one of the fuel tanks to

131
00:11:07,859 --> 00:11:08,559
explode.

132
00:11:09,180 --> 00:11:14,700
The recovery of Flight 800 called into service a significant portion of the Navy's salvage assets.

133
00:11:16,440 --> 00:11:25,800
But it was back when salvage technologies were not as sophisticated that the Navy faced one of its biggest challenges, to resurrect much of the Pacific fleet.

134
00:11:26,820 --> 00:11:30,660
after the attack that swept the United States into World War II.

135
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:36,740
During the nine-month-long Flight 800 recovery operation,

136
00:11:37,300 --> 00:11:40,740
the divers involved made over 4,300 dives.

137
00:11:42,080 --> 00:11:45,240
Deep Sea Salvage will return on Modern Marvels.

138
00:11:51,600 --> 00:11:54,440
Navy Salvage divers working on the ocean floor

139
00:11:54,441 --> 00:11:56,980
couldn't perform the important work that they do

140
00:11:56,981 --> 00:11:58,280
without support from above.

141
00:12:00,280 --> 00:12:02,680
Deep-sea salvage jobs in the open seas

142
00:12:02,681 --> 00:12:04,280
require a base of operations

143
00:12:04,281 --> 00:12:06,580
of one or more ships or barges,

144
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:09,520
referred to as platforms in the salvage business.

145
00:12:11,600 --> 00:12:12,900
The pride of the fleet

146
00:12:12,901 --> 00:12:17,200
are four Safeguard ARS-50 class rescue and salvage ships.

147
00:12:17,940 --> 00:12:23,520
Four ships are USS Grasp, Grapple, Safeguard, and Salvor.

148
00:12:23,521 --> 00:12:31,520
The Grasp and Grapple are both stationed in Little Creek, Virginia, and they primarily operate in the Atlantic and Mediterranean.

149
00:12:32,620 --> 00:12:35,360
The Salvor is stationed in Pearl Harbor, Hawaii.

150
00:12:35,820 --> 00:12:40,300
We operate in the Pacific from California to Southeast Asia.

151
00:12:41,080 --> 00:12:47,520
Safeguard is stationed in Sasebo, Japan, and they operate from Japan all the way to the Indian Ocean.

152
00:12:49,520 --> 00:12:53,340
Each Safeguard-class ship is 255 feet long.

153
00:12:53,520 --> 00:12:56,400
and weighs in at nearly 3,300 tons.

154
00:12:57,160 --> 00:12:59,980
The engine room drives two propulsion shafts

155
00:12:59,981 --> 00:13:02,380
that combine to produce 4,200 horsepower.

156
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,720
Top speed is 14.5 knots.

157
00:13:07,800 --> 00:13:09,240
Built in the mid-1980s,

158
00:13:09,580 --> 00:13:11,120
each ship's bridge has been modified

159
00:13:11,121 --> 00:13:12,940
with up-to-date navigational equipment.

160
00:13:15,300 --> 00:13:16,540
Here on the grapple,

161
00:13:16,680 --> 00:13:19,120
I'm standing in front of the 3-centimeter radar

162
00:13:19,121 --> 00:13:21,500
that we use to track all the vessels

163
00:13:21,501 --> 00:13:23,340
in our immediate vicinity,

164
00:13:23,341 --> 00:13:29,220
within 48 miles, and we also use it for extra help on tracking our course.

165
00:13:29,700 --> 00:13:33,900
In conjunction with our ECTUS, which is an electronic chart computer,

166
00:13:34,380 --> 00:13:36,700
it gives us information from these radars,

167
00:13:36,820 --> 00:13:40,140
it gets input from the automatic ship identification system,

168
00:13:40,240 --> 00:13:42,900
it gets GPS input, gives me depths,

169
00:13:42,901 --> 00:13:45,740
it gives me all contours of shoreline,

170
00:13:46,360 --> 00:13:48,500
and we back up the ECTUS with our paper chart.

171
00:13:49,900 --> 00:13:52,060
To facilitate salvage operations,

172
00:13:52,061 --> 00:13:54,360
each end of the ship is outfitted with a boom.

173
00:13:56,660 --> 00:13:59,620
The forward boom has a 7.5-ton capacity,

174
00:14:00,060 --> 00:14:03,780
but the heavier lifting is done from the back of the ship.

175
00:14:04,440 --> 00:14:06,540
Above us, we have a 40-ton boom

176
00:14:06,541 --> 00:14:08,560
with a main hook and an auxiliary hook.

177
00:14:08,820 --> 00:14:11,600
The main hook is able to lift 40 tons,

178
00:14:11,920 --> 00:14:13,640
the auxiliary hook 5.5 tons.

179
00:14:14,160 --> 00:14:17,240
Primarily use that if we're going to lift

180
00:14:17,241 --> 00:14:20,460
a piece of salvage equipment up onto the fan tail

181
00:14:20,461 --> 00:14:25,160
or if we have large pieces of equipment on the fantail we need to move around, we can use it for that.

182
00:14:26,740 --> 00:14:30,600
The boom is also used to hoist the large hatch in the center of the fantail

183
00:14:30,601 --> 00:14:34,500
to gain access to the salvage equipment stored in the cargo hold below.

184
00:14:37,940 --> 00:14:42,600
Inside, each ship also houses a machine shop to support on-site operations.

185
00:14:43,920 --> 00:14:49,200
Large drums hold 3,000 feet of 2-1⁄4-inch thick wire rope for salvaging and towing.

186
00:14:49,201 --> 00:14:53,960
The Safeguard class of rescue and salvage ship was designed to tow a

187
00:14:53,961 --> 00:14:59,200
Numis-class aircraft carrier at around five knots.

188
00:14:59,201 --> 00:15:06,100
We have the ability to conduct a lift and we can use the bow and stern rollers to pick

189
00:15:06,101 --> 00:15:10,800
up 300 tons off the bottom of the ocean.

190
00:15:10,801 --> 00:15:16,200
The ships are also outfitted with multiple anchors and four enormous orange buoys that

191
00:15:16,201 --> 00:15:19,160
are used to securely moor the ship over an underwater job site.

192
00:15:19,161 --> 00:15:22,667
There are two on each side. Those are called spring buoys.

193
00:15:22,668 --> 00:15:24,300
And we use those at the end

194
00:15:24,301 --> 00:15:29,880
of a beach gear leg, which is an anchor, length of chain, and then wire that would come up

195
00:15:29,881 --> 00:15:31,209
and attach to the buoy.

196
00:15:31,210 --> 00:15:35,140
Once we lay the legs of beach gear, the buoys would remain in place.

197
00:15:35,141 --> 00:15:39,040
And we would attach to those buoys with large mooring lines.

198
00:15:39,041 --> 00:15:40,860
And we can adjust the length

199
00:15:40,861 --> 00:15:44,040
of those lines to move us around inside the moor.

200
00:15:44,041 --> 00:15:50,320
The advantage of a four-point mower is you can move around a large area to conduct diving operations.

201
00:15:52,540 --> 00:15:55,960
Safeguard-class ships not only fully support the divers assigned to them,

202
00:15:56,300 --> 00:15:59,540
but also carry full firefighting gear for the crew and divers.

203
00:16:01,720 --> 00:16:05,740
The crews can use fire monitors, which are essentially water cannons

204
00:16:05,741 --> 00:16:08,540
that spray seawater at up to 1,000 gallons per minute.

205
00:16:09,880 --> 00:16:13,700
The Safeguard-class ships were built to modernize the Navy's salvage fleet.

206
00:16:14,040 --> 00:16:18,260
which up until that point was still heavily relying on World War II-era ships.

207
00:16:20,040 --> 00:16:24,320
Another vintage asset retired about the same time were the dive suits

208
00:16:24,321 --> 00:16:27,000
that looked like something out of a Jules Verne novel.

209
00:16:27,800 --> 00:16:32,660
The classic Mark V dive helmet and suit underwent some alterations over the years,

210
00:16:32,860 --> 00:16:38,820
but this was basically the gear that divers suited up in from the 1890s to the 1980s.

211
00:16:40,340 --> 00:16:42,560
It's very bulky. It's very safe.

212
00:16:42,561 --> 00:16:44,060
It was, believe it or not.

213
00:16:44,200 --> 00:16:47,780
It's really good for going inside wrecks, working in there, things like that,

214
00:16:47,800 --> 00:16:52,180
because you have the hard hat, you have a nice thick canvas protection over the top of you.

215
00:16:52,400 --> 00:16:55,520
But the whole rig's roughly about 194 to 198 pounds.

216
00:16:56,140 --> 00:16:57,240
It's a good rig.

217
00:16:57,320 --> 00:16:58,760
It's solid for working on the inside.

218
00:16:58,880 --> 00:17:00,500
It wasn't for this rig in the Pearl Harbor.

219
00:17:00,680 --> 00:17:03,540
We never got the ships up the ones that we did.

220
00:17:05,280 --> 00:17:06,100
Pearl Harbor.

221
00:17:06,760 --> 00:17:11,260
It was here in 1941 that Navy divers donned their Mark Vs

222
00:17:11,261 --> 00:17:14,560
to do their part in the most massive salvage challenge in U.S. history.

223
00:17:15,840 --> 00:17:18,640
It's a nice morning here at Pearl Harbor in the Hawaiian Islands.

224
00:17:19,780 --> 00:17:22,560
People are queued up to go out and see the USS Arizona Memorial.

225
00:17:25,600 --> 00:17:29,440
But in 1941, on a Sunday morning, the whole place was in flames.

226
00:17:29,680 --> 00:17:32,120
It was chaos. Ships were sinking. People were dying.

227
00:17:32,760 --> 00:17:35,760
The Imperial Navy had attacked the U.S. fleet stationed here at Pearl Harbor.

228
00:17:36,100 --> 00:17:41,300
After the attack was over, they found that 21 vessels had been sunk or damaged.

229
00:17:41,301 --> 00:17:46,040
Seven of those vessels moored along the edge of Fort Island, the great battleships, the main battle line.

230
00:17:46,580 --> 00:17:52,580
The fires did burn out of control, but fortunately, the shipyard and the dry docks were intact.

231
00:17:52,900 --> 00:17:57,720
So the mechanics and the facilities were available to raise this fleet.

232
00:17:58,460 --> 00:18:01,660
You can imagine what it was like for divers to work in this environment.

233
00:18:02,260 --> 00:18:04,500
The waters of Pearl Harbor were filled with oil.

234
00:18:04,501 --> 00:18:05,700
There was debris.

235
00:18:06,340 --> 00:18:09,300
There were bodies from the ships that had been thrown overboard.

236
00:18:10,220 --> 00:18:12,720
After the initial rescue and recovery efforts,

237
00:18:13,340 --> 00:18:17,420
the salvage crews first dealt with the ships that suffered relatively little damage.

238
00:18:18,020 --> 00:18:21,060
Underwater patches to the hulls and pumping operations

239
00:18:21,061 --> 00:18:23,760
got several vessels either back into service

240
00:18:23,761 --> 00:18:26,500
or able to make it to dry dock for final repairs.

241
00:18:27,760 --> 00:18:31,340
Within a few months, several ships returned to active duty.

242
00:18:31,341 --> 00:18:34,680
others required more extensive repairs,

243
00:18:34,681 --> 00:18:38,520
but it was the capsized vessels that presented bigger challenges.

244
00:18:38,521 --> 00:18:43,620
The Japanese force that came in from the west made a torpedo attack

245
00:18:43,621 --> 00:18:46,020
on one of the ships, the mine layer Oglala.

246
00:18:46,021 --> 00:18:48,560
Because of the shallow draft of that vessel,

247
00:18:48,561 --> 00:18:50,660
the torpedo actually traveled under the hull,

248
00:18:50,661 --> 00:18:52,660
striking the Helena behind it.

249
00:18:52,661 --> 00:18:55,300
Well, the Helena exploded,

250
00:18:55,301 --> 00:18:58,940
and the shockwaves from the torpedoes burst seams in the Oglala.

251
00:18:58,941 --> 00:19:01,980
And she took on water slowly and sank.

252
00:19:02,180 --> 00:19:04,920
She just turned sideways and went down.

253
00:19:06,280 --> 00:19:07,880
Veterans like to say she died of fright.

254
00:19:08,920 --> 00:19:11,220
The salvage team's unusual challenge

255
00:19:11,221 --> 00:19:14,540
was to roll over and refloat the massive old mine layer.

256
00:19:15,460 --> 00:19:17,880
The solution involved the use of a pontoon system,

257
00:19:18,420 --> 00:19:20,440
originally devised to raise sunken submarines

258
00:19:20,441 --> 00:19:21,660
and ships off the bottom.

259
00:19:22,540 --> 00:19:24,500
The idea of the salvage pontoon

260
00:19:24,501 --> 00:19:27,620
is to float them to either side of the sunken vessel.

261
00:19:27,621 --> 00:19:33,900
You then fill them with water, drop them down, pass cables underneath the vessel that sunk,

262
00:19:34,160 --> 00:19:36,720
and secure these pontoons to either side.

263
00:19:37,760 --> 00:19:40,460
Once these were on the bottom next to the ship,

264
00:19:40,720 --> 00:19:45,560
they would bring hoses along to pump out the water and fill these now with air.

265
00:19:45,940 --> 00:19:52,180
This gradual process allows these pontoons to be used as a lifting body to bring that ship up to the surface,

266
00:19:52,580 --> 00:19:55,620
so these act as life vests around the ship.

267
00:19:55,621 --> 00:20:02,940
But the Oglala, lying on its side in the harbor, required a new and untested approach to using

268
00:20:02,941 --> 00:20:03,841
the pontoons.

269
00:20:03,842 --> 00:20:08,900
One was positioned at the bow, three on the stern, and six on one side of

270
00:20:08,901 --> 00:20:09,646
the vessel.

271
00:20:09,647 --> 00:20:15,000
Chains were attached to the pontoons along the side, passed under the ship, and

272
00:20:15,001 --> 00:20:19,650
secured to the starboard side. Then the pontoons were pumped full of air.

273
00:20:19,651 --> 00:20:20,860
As they rose to the

274
00:20:20,861 --> 00:20:24,691
the surface, the chains rolled the ship back over.

275
00:20:24,692 --> 00:20:27,680
The first riding attempt failed because

276
00:20:27,681 --> 00:20:30,225
some of the chains broke loose.

277
00:20:30,226 --> 00:20:35,560
But 12 days later, on April 23, 1942, the Oglala was successfully

278
00:20:35,561 --> 00:20:41,540
pulled upright. Divers could now patch the damaged hull. The water was pumped out of

279
00:20:41,541 --> 00:20:47,300
the ship so it could be moved to dry dock, and soon, the Oglala was seaworthy again.

280
00:20:47,301 --> 00:20:54,960
Today, the USS Missouri, the battleship on which the Japanese surrender was signed, is moored on Pearl Harbor's Ford Island.

281
00:20:55,780 --> 00:21:00,220
But in 1941, this is where the USS Oklahoma was attacked.

282
00:21:01,620 --> 00:21:04,780
After being hit by several torpedoes, she capsized.

283
00:21:06,540 --> 00:21:11,940
The Oklahoma's elaborate salvage plan involved the installation of 21 large witches on Ford Island,

284
00:21:11,941 --> 00:21:15,180
an equal number of towers attached to the ship itself

285
00:21:15,181 --> 00:21:19,240
and steel cables rigged between ship and shore.

286
00:21:21,280 --> 00:21:25,320
Essential to the riding of the Oklahoma were electric motors.

287
00:21:25,980 --> 00:21:28,620
And what they came up with was a recycled motor

288
00:21:28,621 --> 00:21:30,960
that was once used on the Honolulu trolleys.

289
00:21:31,780 --> 00:21:35,660
They devised a way that they could pull the ship over slowly

290
00:21:35,661 --> 00:21:39,060
over a period of days, months, almost a year.

291
00:21:39,061 --> 00:21:43,880
Without that motor, that slow process could never be done.

292
00:21:44,620 --> 00:21:47,880
The ship came afloat in early November 1943

293
00:21:47,881 --> 00:21:50,920
and was sent to dry dock for final repairs.

294
00:21:51,620 --> 00:21:52,860
After this Herculean effort,

295
00:21:53,180 --> 00:21:56,560
the Oklahoma was deemed too old and damaged to return to service.

296
00:21:58,440 --> 00:22:00,720
There were never plans to raise the Arizona,

297
00:22:01,240 --> 00:22:05,280
and she remains beneath the memorial to those who perished during the attack.

298
00:22:05,281 --> 00:22:11,220
In the assessment of this battleship, they found that she was unservable.

299
00:22:11,700 --> 00:22:13,200
Her damage was so extensive.

300
00:22:13,620 --> 00:22:18,380
But she was salvageable, and they would take the upper works or her superstructure,

301
00:22:18,540 --> 00:22:22,560
cut it away, and place it in a scrapyard so they could be used in the war effort.

302
00:22:22,980 --> 00:22:25,640
But the two aft turrets, turrets number three and four,

303
00:22:26,040 --> 00:22:30,500
those were taken from the ship and placed as coastal guns here in Hawaii.

304
00:22:30,501 --> 00:22:32,300
So they did serve another purpose.

305
00:22:32,301 --> 00:22:37,560
But the other part of it was that the crew, nearly 900 men, still rest inside that ship.

306
00:22:40,560 --> 00:22:44,760
The Pearl Harbor salvage operations helped alter the course of history.

307
00:22:45,780 --> 00:22:50,240
But one recent spectacular salvage project was not about changing history.

308
00:22:50,840 --> 00:22:52,600
It was about preserving it.

309
00:22:54,440 --> 00:22:59,000
The effort to salvage the damaged ship stationed at Pearl Harbor in 1941

310
00:22:59,001 --> 00:23:04,360
required Navy and civilian divers to spend 20,000 hours underwater.

311
00:23:05,420 --> 00:23:07,920
Deep Sea Salvage will return on Modern Marvels.

312
00:23:18,600 --> 00:23:22,160
Salvage divers spend a great deal of time beneath the surface.

313
00:23:22,640 --> 00:23:25,400
They have to rely on their equipment and each other.

314
00:23:25,401 --> 00:23:33,620
Surface Supply Diving is a team effort, especially when dealing with a diver's umbilical, their

315
00:23:33,621 --> 00:23:35,620
lifeline while working below.

316
00:23:35,621 --> 00:23:38,800
Each umbilical is made of three elements.

317
00:23:38,801 --> 00:23:44,420
The L element is your air element, sends air down to the diver so they can breathe.

318
00:23:44,421 --> 00:23:49,280
The blue is your pneumophathometer, and it tells topside how deep the diver is based

319
00:23:49,281 --> 00:23:53,160
on the water pressure, sends air down to the bottom, and based on how much pressure is

320
00:23:53,161 --> 00:23:56,150
down there topside can tell how deep the diver is.

321
00:23:56,151 --> 00:23:59,200
The red is a strength member in your communications

322
00:23:59,201 --> 00:23:59,901
cable.

323
00:23:59,902 --> 00:24:05,660
It allows topside to talk to the divers and it also supports the weight of the diver.

324
00:24:05,661 --> 00:24:10,740
Another piece of dive equipment always close by during deep dive operations is a recompression

325
00:24:10,741 --> 00:24:11,441
chamber.

326
00:24:11,442 --> 00:24:18,600
It's designed to remedy decompression issues, including the condition commonly called

327
00:24:18,601 --> 00:24:22,000
the bends.

328
00:24:22,001 --> 00:24:26,800
If a diver comes up too fast, the gases that have been pressurized in his body

329
00:24:26,801 --> 00:24:29,880
can create bubbles in his bloodstream.

330
00:24:29,881 --> 00:24:36,600
A simple comparison is opening a carbonated soft drink.

331
00:24:36,601 --> 00:24:41,540
The bubbles appear in the liquid when the pressure is released.

332
00:24:41,541 --> 00:24:45,160
A light case of the bends may cause joint pain.

333
00:24:45,161 --> 00:24:51,480
Worst cases involve bubbles that lodge in the spinal cord or brain and can cause paralysis or death.

334
00:24:52,120 --> 00:24:57,980
In a recompression chamber, a diver is repressurized so the bubbles dissolve back into the diver's blood,

335
00:24:58,240 --> 00:25:01,160
and then he is slowly brought back to normal surface pressure.

336
00:25:05,280 --> 00:25:12,220
Emergency decompression issues are kept to a minimum for Navy divers by the careful adherence to the guidelines on the established dive tables.

337
00:25:12,221 --> 00:25:19,220
These rules dictate how deep they can go, for how long, and what gas they should be breathing.

338
00:25:19,221 --> 00:25:27,220
It's the Navy Experimental Diving Unit, or NEDU, located in Florida, that keeps divers safe.

339
00:25:27,221 --> 00:25:33,220
It's the Center for Military Diving Research and Equipment Testing and Evaluation.

340
00:25:33,221 --> 00:25:38,220
Any time the Navy's interested in a piece of equipment or want to modify something they already have,

341
00:25:38,221 --> 00:25:41,980
we first get that equipment down here in the unmanned testing department.

342
00:25:42,520 --> 00:25:45,760
One of the things we have is a mannequin head, which we can instrument.

343
00:25:45,900 --> 00:25:47,820
We can put sensors in to measure various things,

344
00:25:48,160 --> 00:25:53,120
and then we would take the hyperbaric chamber to various depths by compressing it with air.

345
00:25:54,540 --> 00:26:00,460
We have breathing machines there that can simulate lungs that no human on Earth has,

346
00:26:00,700 --> 00:26:05,240
and we breathe them at every temperature and at every breathing rate

347
00:26:05,241 --> 00:26:07,460
with every kind of gas that we ever expect to put in them.

348
00:26:07,461 --> 00:26:16,060
Before we put it on a guy, we want to make sure that structurally, design-wise, and every other way, shape, and form we can tell,

349
00:26:16,300 --> 00:26:19,820
this is a solid piece of equipment that's been designed well and is designed to function.

350
00:26:20,640 --> 00:26:24,120
The NEDU not only devotes a great deal of attention to equipment,

351
00:26:24,620 --> 00:26:28,800
but also focuses on the biomedical effects of deep-sea dives on the human body.

352
00:26:29,540 --> 00:26:32,940
It tests and observes these effects in its ocean simulation facility.

353
00:26:32,941 --> 00:26:42,640
The structure is designed to have a five-chamber habitat on the upper level connected by a trunk,

354
00:26:43,040 --> 00:26:50,040
and at the bottom of the trunk is a 55,000-gallon, 47-foot-long wet pot.

355
00:26:50,980 --> 00:26:53,240
The entire structure is made out of HY80 steel.

356
00:26:54,360 --> 00:27:02,080
In the ocean simulation facility, temperature control ranges from 28 degrees Fahrenheit all the way up to 105 degrees.

357
00:27:02,081 --> 00:27:05,680
Stand up and unhat.

358
00:27:05,780 --> 00:27:08,480
After air or gas is pumped into the sealed chamber,

359
00:27:08,580 --> 00:27:11,080
the atmosphere in the habitats on the upper level

360
00:27:11,180 --> 00:27:14,920
and in the wet pot below can be pressurized to replicate depths

361
00:27:15,020 --> 00:27:17,420
up to 2,250 feet.

362
00:27:17,520 --> 00:27:19,540
All divers, when you're ready to travel,

363
00:27:19,640 --> 00:27:21,540
give me your keys in front of your horn.

364
00:27:26,680 --> 00:27:28,680
50 feet.

365
00:27:28,780 --> 00:27:30,580
60 feet.

366
00:27:30,581 --> 00:27:35,060
The NEDU is instrumental in the development of mixed gas diving

367
00:27:35,061 --> 00:27:38,760
and continues to analyze the benefits of different gas ratios.

368
00:27:39,700 --> 00:27:44,120
We have a storage capacity of over a million standard cubic foot of gas.

369
00:27:44,500 --> 00:27:48,780
We use air, helium, and oxygen, and nitrogen.

370
00:27:49,420 --> 00:27:52,220
We have the capabilities of mixing all those gases together

371
00:27:52,221 --> 00:27:55,820
to obtain whatever mixture we need for a particular dive,

372
00:27:56,480 --> 00:27:58,960
usually driven by whatever depth we're doing

373
00:27:58,961 --> 00:28:01,240
or the particular type of equipment that we're using.

374
00:28:02,800 --> 00:28:05,660
To properly test the equipment and gas combinations,

375
00:28:06,320 --> 00:28:08,520
the divers ride bikes while in the water.

376
00:28:08,760 --> 00:28:11,800
The bikes, we put 50 to 60 watts of resistance on it.

377
00:28:12,020 --> 00:28:14,420
It makes the divers breathe harder while they're on the bottom,

378
00:28:14,660 --> 00:28:18,640
and it simulates work that would be done on the bottom on a real job.

379
00:28:20,340 --> 00:28:23,160
As a result of the research done by the NEDU,

380
00:28:23,900 --> 00:28:27,920
Navy regulations require that its divers working below 190 feet

381
00:28:27,921 --> 00:28:29,049
breathe mixed gas.

382
00:28:29,050 --> 00:28:33,440
The normal air we breathe is primarily made up of nitrogen and oxygen,

383
00:28:33,980 --> 00:28:39,720
but when the human body is under the pressure of a deep dive, it processes these gases differently.

384
00:28:40,980 --> 00:28:46,240
Nitrogen can cause a disorienting feeling like drunkenness, and oxygen's adverse effects include

385
00:28:46,241 --> 00:28:53,560
nausea and convulsions. For deep dives, a combination of helium and less oxygen than

386
00:28:53,561 --> 00:28:58,240
in normal air has proven most beneficial.

387
00:28:58,241 --> 00:29:01,240
Navy divers were able to utilize the advantages of mixed gas

388
00:29:01,241 --> 00:29:04,000
diving during the mission to recover artifacts

389
00:29:04,001 --> 00:29:07,640
from the hallowed Civil War vessel, the Union's USS

390
00:29:07,641 --> 00:29:08,341
Monitor.

391
00:29:12,420 --> 00:29:15,220
It was nine months after its famous battle with the Merrimack

392
00:29:15,221 --> 00:29:19,920
in March 1862 that the Monitor, with its innovative rotating

393
00:29:19,921 --> 00:29:22,660
gun turret, sank off the coast of North Carolina.

394
00:29:23,560 --> 00:29:33,000
In 1998, working with the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration, Navy divers,

395
00:29:33,001 --> 00:29:37,500
breathing a mixture of helium and oxygen, began artifact recovery from the monitor's

396
00:29:37,501 --> 00:29:41,660
resting place, 240 feet below the surface.

397
00:29:41,661 --> 00:29:49,560
Just diving on the monitor, especially for a naval officer, is a pretty emotional dive.

398
00:29:49,561 --> 00:29:54,880
You see this Civil War ironclad ship that really changed the course of naval history

399
00:29:54,881 --> 00:30:00,120
and you're walking the decks of your naval ancestors.

400
00:30:00,121 --> 00:30:05,260
Because the Monitor project was of such archaeological importance, all Navy divers wore helmet-mounted

401
00:30:05,261 --> 00:30:10,180
cameras so those above could see what was happening.

402
00:30:10,181 --> 00:30:14,060
The helmet cameras on the Navy's dive suit was incredibly beneficial to the archaeological

403
00:30:14,061 --> 00:30:17,780
team because we weren't allowed to dive with the Navy, so there had to be an incredible

404
00:30:17,781 --> 00:30:20,680
amount of trust factor to what was going on.

405
00:30:20,681 --> 00:30:24,400
We got to the point where we were removing something or cutting something, where to cut

406
00:30:24,401 --> 00:30:29,900
and how to cut it, and it really got to that degree where each diver was really working

407
00:30:29,901 --> 00:30:35,960
with the archaeologists and actually working as the hands for the archaeologists.

408
00:30:35,961 --> 00:30:42,900
In the summer of 2002, the Navy divers were ready to help raise the Monitor's gun turret.

409
00:30:42,901 --> 00:30:47,020
First they had to remove a section of the Monitor's hull and armor belt that had covered

410
00:30:47,021 --> 00:30:49,900
the gun turret since the ship capsized in 1862.

411
00:30:52,620 --> 00:30:57,100
Next, divers removed the added weight of sand and debris from inside the turret.

412
00:30:58,240 --> 00:31:03,340
Then they attached a custom-built lifting mechanism so that the entire 9-foot-tall,

413
00:31:03,480 --> 00:31:06,980
120-ton turret could be raised to the surface.

414
00:31:08,460 --> 00:31:13,640
This is the actual spider mechanism that was designed and built specifically for this operation.

415
00:31:14,640 --> 00:31:16,740
Now, when the spider was lowered down,

416
00:31:17,200 --> 00:31:20,700
each of these legs would be extended out 15 degrees

417
00:31:20,701 --> 00:31:23,800
so that when we lowered the spider onto the turret,

418
00:31:23,940 --> 00:31:27,040
we didn't have a danger of banging up the turret or damaging it.

419
00:31:28,060 --> 00:31:31,400
And then utilizing hand-operated hydraulics by the diver

420
00:31:31,401 --> 00:31:35,660
that would bring the legs back into the position like this

421
00:31:35,661 --> 00:31:40,940
that you see here so that the feet were underneath the lower lip of the turret to bring her up.

422
00:31:40,941 --> 00:31:48,760
On August 5th, workers using a 500-ton capacity crane lifted the turret out of the water and

423
00:31:48,761 --> 00:31:55,940
secured it to the deck of a barge for transport to the Mariner's Museum in Newport News,

424
00:31:55,941 --> 00:31:57,440
Virginia.

425
00:31:57,441 --> 00:32:02,000
Some of the contents recovered from inside the turret included two 11-inch Dahlgren

426
00:32:02,001 --> 00:32:07,080
cannons along with some skeletal remains and artifacts from a few of the men that went

427
00:32:07,081 --> 00:32:12,080
down with the ship nearly 150 years ago.

428
00:32:12,081 --> 00:32:14,940
The Monitor project lasted five years,

429
00:32:14,941 --> 00:32:20,200
allowing plenty of time to plan and build specialized equipment.

430
00:32:20,201 --> 00:32:22,740
But many salvage missions are crises

431
00:32:22,741 --> 00:32:25,940
that need to be dealt with immediately.

432
00:32:25,941 --> 00:32:29,020
Two even posed the threat of nuclear disaster.

433
00:32:32,060 --> 00:32:34,560
Navy divers made their first operational use

434
00:32:34,561 --> 00:32:38,780
of a helium-oxygen breathing gas mixture in 1939

435
00:32:38,781 --> 00:32:42,380
as they salvaged the submarine USS Squalus.

436
00:32:42,381 --> 00:32:44,940
Deep Sea Salvage will return on Modern Marvels.

437
00:32:51,380 --> 00:32:54,180
One of the major challenges of deep sea salvage

438
00:32:54,181 --> 00:32:58,620
has always been locating an object on the ocean floor.

439
00:32:58,621 --> 00:33:00,700
Wind and currents may have carried clues

440
00:33:00,701 --> 00:33:04,780
like floating debris for miles.

441
00:33:04,781 --> 00:33:07,180
An underwater search often begins with the deployment

442
00:33:07,181 --> 00:33:11,920
of a side-scan sonar, referred to as a towfish.

443
00:33:11,921 --> 00:33:14,380
It is designed to glide above the ocean floor

444
00:33:14,381 --> 00:33:17,620
so it can scan the bottom while pulled by a cable attached

445
00:33:17,621 --> 00:33:19,100
to the ship above.

446
00:33:19,101 --> 00:33:21,540
What you're seeing here is the topside console

447
00:33:21,541 --> 00:33:25,360
of the operator's console for the side-scan sonar system.

448
00:33:25,361 --> 00:33:27,740
And this represents state-of-the-art.

449
00:33:27,741 --> 00:33:32,380
And what you see here is information as it would be seen from the towfish.

450
00:33:32,920 --> 00:33:35,280
You see the center would be the towfish.

451
00:33:35,460 --> 00:33:38,180
Out to each side represents 150 meters.

452
00:33:38,980 --> 00:33:43,980
We spend 12 hours a day every day for up to, well, as long as it takes,

453
00:33:43,981 --> 00:33:49,400
a month, two months, to look for one red blob on a screen.

454
00:33:49,860 --> 00:33:52,100
This particular blob is a sunken helicopter.

455
00:33:53,120 --> 00:33:57,320
But side-scan sonars can capture images of metal objects as small as a quarter.

456
00:33:57,740 --> 00:34:06,340
Once the object is found, ROVs and submersibles now make it possible to conduct salvage operations

457
00:34:06,341 --> 00:34:09,180
thousands of feet below the surface.

458
00:34:09,181 --> 00:34:15,180
But during a crisis involving a lost nuclear submarine in the early 1960s, the Navy had

459
00:34:15,181 --> 00:34:19,860
only one way to go deep, the Trieste.

460
00:34:19,861 --> 00:34:24,136
Now on display in Washington, D.C.'s National Museum of the U.S.

461
00:34:24,137 --> 00:34:26,140
Navy, the Bathyscaphe Trieste,

462
00:34:26,141 --> 00:34:31,540
by a Swiss scientist, was purchased by the US Navy in 1958.

463
00:34:31,541 --> 00:34:35,140
The upper portion is made of fuel and water ballast tanks,

464
00:34:35,141 --> 00:34:37,240
while the operators are housed in the small bell

465
00:34:37,241 --> 00:34:40,640
on the bottom of the craft.

466
00:34:40,641 --> 00:34:43,940
The Trieste was used in a series of historic deep submergence

467
00:34:43,941 --> 00:34:46,940
tests in the Pacific Ocean, including a dive

468
00:34:46,941 --> 00:34:51,240
in the Marianas Trench, the deepest spot on the ocean floor,

469
00:34:51,241 --> 00:34:56,440
to the record depth of 35,798 feet.

470
00:34:58,440 --> 00:35:00,500
After just 20 minutes of that depth,

471
00:35:00,920 --> 00:35:04,520
the operators began their ascent by releasing the weight of the iron pellets

472
00:35:04,521 --> 00:35:06,780
held in two hopper-like silos.

473
00:35:08,400 --> 00:35:13,960
In April 1963, a missing atomic submarine, the USS Thresher,

474
00:35:14,640 --> 00:35:16,680
sank in about 8,000 feet of water.

475
00:35:17,600 --> 00:35:20,740
At that point, there were no other submersibles that could dive that deep,

476
00:35:20,741 --> 00:35:22,120
other than the Trieste.

477
00:35:23,320 --> 00:35:27,400
The enormous bathyscap was transported to the east coast of the United States

478
00:35:27,401 --> 00:35:32,360
to aid in the critical search taking place about 200 miles off the coast of Massachusetts.

479
00:35:34,780 --> 00:35:37,260
The thresher had imploded on its way to the bottom,

480
00:35:37,620 --> 00:35:42,960
so the immediate concern was that the nuclear reactor in the submarine's engine room might have ruptured.

481
00:35:43,400 --> 00:35:49,260
Based off of photographs that Trieste was able to take of nuclear submarine thresher

482
00:35:49,261 --> 00:35:54,600
and the few pieces of the submarine that Trieste was able to recover and bring to the surface,

483
00:35:54,740 --> 00:35:59,320
the Navy determined that there was a massive pipe failure on board the submarine,

484
00:35:59,600 --> 00:36:02,080
and that's what caused Thresher to sink.

485
00:36:04,060 --> 00:36:09,320
Radiation detection equipment confirmed that the nuclear material in the submarine's reactor was still contained.

486
00:36:10,380 --> 00:36:13,000
The Navy decided to leave the submarine on the bottom.

487
00:36:14,820 --> 00:36:18,200
Subsequent tests over the years have shown no radiation leakage.

488
00:36:19,260 --> 00:36:23,020
Trieste was decommissioned shortly after its successful search for the thresher.

489
00:36:23,900 --> 00:36:27,240
It was replaced by the new Alvin submersible.

490
00:36:27,840 --> 00:36:31,980
The Alvin was not designed to operate in the same extreme depths as the Trieste,

491
00:36:32,380 --> 00:36:36,680
but was much more maneuverable and better equipped for search and salvage operations.

492
00:36:38,200 --> 00:36:42,040
Luckily for the Navy, it had completed its initial tests just in time

493
00:36:42,041 --> 00:36:45,220
to aid in a potentially even more disastrous nuclear crisis.

494
00:36:45,560 --> 00:36:47,840
This time, it was a missing H-bomb.

495
00:36:47,841 --> 00:36:59,240
On January 17, 1966, a B-52 bomber flying over the coast of Spain collided with its KC-135 tanker.

496
00:37:00,340 --> 00:37:06,640
During that collision, four hydrogen bombs were released and dropped to the coastline of Spain.

497
00:37:07,440 --> 00:37:10,480
Three of those bombs were found almost immediately.

498
00:37:12,420 --> 00:37:16,360
The bombs were not armed, so there was no chance of a nuclear detonation.

499
00:37:17,320 --> 00:37:23,100
Nevertheless, loose nukes posed just as frightening a problem in 1966 as they do today.

500
00:37:24,160 --> 00:37:28,660
After combing the countryside, search teams found no trace of bomb number four.

501
00:37:29,040 --> 00:37:32,800
There was an important clue to the location of that bomb.

502
00:37:32,801 --> 00:37:39,520
A fisherman had seen a parachute pass over his boat, dangling what he thought was a man, a dead man,

503
00:37:39,840 --> 00:37:42,600
and then descend into the water near his boat.

504
00:37:42,601 --> 00:37:47,600
The Navy began a major underwater search.

505
00:37:47,700 --> 00:37:50,100
Divers combed the shallow waters,

506
00:37:50,200 --> 00:37:52,600
and the Alvin and other submersibles

507
00:37:52,700 --> 00:37:56,140
scanned the ocean floor below diver depths.

508
00:37:56,240 --> 00:38:00,060
Very soon after that, Alvin noticed a track

509
00:38:00,160 --> 00:38:03,060
where it was searching that looked like a torpedo

510
00:38:03,160 --> 00:38:05,860
had been dragged over the bottom.

511
00:38:05,960 --> 00:38:08,060
After weeks of surveying and photographing

512
00:38:08,160 --> 00:38:12,600
the ocean floor, the Alvin's crew located the bomb.

513
00:38:12,601 --> 00:38:17,380
sitting on a steep slope, 2,550 feet below the surface.

514
00:38:18,100 --> 00:38:21,320
After the H-bomb was found on the 15th of March,

515
00:38:21,500 --> 00:38:23,640
an attempt was made to recover it.

516
00:38:23,980 --> 00:38:28,820
Alvin went down with a line and attempted to attach this line,

517
00:38:29,380 --> 00:38:32,060
wrap it into the shrouds of the parachute.

518
00:38:32,400 --> 00:38:34,940
Unfortunately, when the lift attempt was made,

519
00:38:34,941 --> 00:38:40,400
the line parted and the bomb once again dropped into the depths.

520
00:38:42,600 --> 00:38:48,040
This time, the Navy decided to use an unmanned device originally designed for torpedo recovery,

521
00:38:48,820 --> 00:38:52,960
called the Cable-Controlled Underwater Recovery Vehicle, or CURVE.

522
00:38:53,660 --> 00:38:56,140
It would attempt to attach grapnels to the chute.

523
00:38:56,920 --> 00:39:04,740
Unfortunately, CURVE itself got tangled in the parachute, and a decision had to be made to lift it.

524
00:39:04,741 --> 00:39:09,340
That moment was so tense that on board the recovery ship,

525
00:39:09,780 --> 00:39:14,220
one of the individuals that was in charge of curve fainted dead away.

526
00:39:14,221 --> 00:39:18,120
But the lift was made, curve was brought near the surface,

527
00:39:18,700 --> 00:39:22,640
divers were sent down to secure much heavier lines on it,

528
00:39:22,980 --> 00:39:24,780
and then it was finally covered.

529
00:39:25,420 --> 00:39:27,600
Bomb number four was out of the ocean.

530
00:39:28,780 --> 00:39:32,280
The 81-day saga of the missing H-bomb was finally over.

531
00:39:32,281 --> 00:39:39,040
Navy divers have proved adept not only at averting nuclear disasters, but also environmental

532
00:39:39,041 --> 00:39:47,080
catastrophes, recently one that was nearly 60 years in the making.

533
00:39:47,081 --> 00:39:51,880
The Elvin Submersible was built by General Mills Electronics Group in the same factory

534
00:39:51,881 --> 00:39:56,840
used to manufacture breakfast cereal producing machinery.

535
00:39:56,841 --> 00:39:59,340
Deep Sea Salvage will return on Modern Marvels.

536
00:40:02,280 --> 00:40:02,980
Ready?

537
00:40:09,980 --> 00:40:13,440
Two highly trained Navy dive teams are dedicated to rescue

538
00:40:13,441 --> 00:40:16,020
and salvage operations.

539
00:40:16,021 --> 00:40:20,620
Mobile diving and salvage units, one and two.

540
00:40:20,621 --> 00:40:24,020
Mud-su-2 operates out of the Little Creek Naval Amphibious

541
00:40:24,021 --> 00:40:27,520
Base near Norfolk, Virginia, close to the safeguard ships

542
00:40:27,521 --> 00:40:29,580
Grasp and Grapple.

543
00:40:29,581 --> 00:40:36,580
Mutsu 1 is stationed in Pearl Harbor, near the salvor.

544
00:40:38,160 --> 00:40:47,160
When not out on a job, team members can be found testing their equipment, and themselves.

545
00:40:48,080 --> 00:40:52,300
The Mutsu teams must keep themselves in a state of constant readiness and prepared for

546
00:40:52,301 --> 00:40:55,340
any contingency.

547
00:40:55,341 --> 00:41:00,260
In deep sea salvage, the one thing that can be counted on is that no two jobs are alike.

548
00:41:01,340 --> 00:41:07,380
Recently, for those stationed in Pearl Harbor, a sunken ship from World War II suddenly demanded attention.

549
00:41:09,440 --> 00:41:16,920
The oil tanker USS Mississinowa went down with over 2 million gallons of oil on board, and it was starting to leak.

550
00:41:17,800 --> 00:41:22,620
In 1944, the oil tanker was anchored northwest of Guam in the Ulithi Lagoon.

551
00:41:25,340 --> 00:41:32,180
On the morning of November 20th, it was struck by a Japanese manned suicide torpedo.

552
00:41:33,500 --> 00:41:39,180
The 553-foot-long tanker containing aviation fuel and oil burst into flames.

553
00:41:39,840 --> 00:41:44,600
The Mississinua slowly sank and took 50 of its crew to their watery grave.

554
00:41:46,300 --> 00:41:53,360
For the next 57 years, the Mississinua, with most of its cargo still held in several undamaged internal tanks,

555
00:41:53,980 --> 00:41:55,100
lay quietly on the bottom.

556
00:41:55,340 --> 00:41:57,740
It is believed that the corroding wreck first began

557
00:41:57,840 --> 00:42:02,640
leaking oil in 2001 during the rough seas caused by a typhoon

558
00:42:02,740 --> 00:42:03,940
that passed through the area.

559
00:42:06,720 --> 00:42:08,880
The discovery of the oil leak caused concern

560
00:42:08,980 --> 00:42:12,400
of an impending environmental disaster.

561
00:42:12,500 --> 00:42:15,500
The whole community of Ulythia Atoll

562
00:42:15,600 --> 00:42:19,600
is a seafaring, very tribal, very small group.

563
00:42:19,700 --> 00:42:23,100
And what we were worried about is oil from that wreck leaking

564
00:42:23,101 --> 00:42:26,140
out and then destroying an entire people's way of life.

565
00:42:26,240 --> 00:42:28,400
Raising the Mississinawa was out of the question

566
00:42:28,500 --> 00:42:30,200
for two reasons.

567
00:42:30,300 --> 00:42:32,540
Trying to move the unstable wreck could cause a major

568
00:42:32,640 --> 00:42:34,540
release of oil.

569
00:42:34,640 --> 00:42:37,340
Plus, the ship was a designated war grave

570
00:42:37,440 --> 00:42:40,360
for those entombed inside.

571
00:42:40,460 --> 00:42:43,460
The Navy decided that the oil had to be removed from the wreck

572
00:42:43,560 --> 00:42:46,660
as it lay on the bottom of the lagoon.

573
00:42:46,760 --> 00:42:50,260
The salvoer moored over the wreck, which lay upside down,

574
00:42:50,360 --> 00:42:52,760
130 feet below.

575
00:42:52,761 --> 00:42:59,220
The main technique the divers used was installing 20 valve assemblies called hot taps to remove the oil.

576
00:43:00,540 --> 00:43:10,440
So what we did was attach the hot tap on the external hull and then finish drilling through the internal tank boundaries until you had oil.

577
00:43:11,680 --> 00:43:17,100
After the divers installed each hot tap, they removed the upper part of the device and attached a hose.

578
00:43:17,101 --> 00:43:26,280
The oil was then pumped up through the hose at the rate of 450 gallons per minute to a barge moored next to the salvoer.

579
00:43:28,340 --> 00:43:35,400
Divers also cut holes in the hull to access two internal oil tanks and removed remaining oil from the engine room and pipes.

580
00:43:36,400 --> 00:43:41,840
We removed a total of 2.1 million gallons of oil, which, you know, a significant amount of oil.

581
00:43:42,160 --> 00:43:43,900
And she was starting to deteriorate.

582
00:43:43,901 --> 00:43:46,620
and that's why they were starting to see oil leak from her,

583
00:43:46,740 --> 00:43:47,940
and it just would have gotten worse.

584
00:43:48,580 --> 00:43:52,900
So, yeah, we eventually averted a pretty significant spill.

585
00:43:54,040 --> 00:43:56,260
What little oil remained in the Mississinawa

586
00:43:56,261 --> 00:43:59,980
no longer posed a threat to the environment or to the people of Ulithi.

587
00:44:01,820 --> 00:44:04,420
The crew of the Salvor and the Mud Sue divers

588
00:44:04,421 --> 00:44:06,960
had helped avert an environmental catastrophe.

589
00:44:07,640 --> 00:44:09,200
They returned home to Pearl Harbor,

590
00:44:10,560 --> 00:44:11,760
knowing that at any moment,

591
00:44:11,761 --> 00:44:17,380
enemy combatants, mechanical failure, or the unpredictable sea

592
00:44:17,381 --> 00:44:21,520
could create yet another candidate for their never-ending mission.

